      <subsection xml:id="subsec-method-implications">
        <title>Methodological Implications</title>

        <p>
          The framework treats reconstruction as part of the measurement model.
          The event log supplies incremental actions, the authored source supplies
          item semantics, and the checked configurations provide internal
          validation anchors.  Keeping these roles separate makes it possible to
          identify where uncertainty enters rather than collapsing all
          preprocessing into an undocumented cleaning step.
        </p>

        <warning>
        <p>
          [STUB: Connect the observed validation and sensitivity results to this
          argument.  Explain which pipeline stages were empirically reliable,
          which warning classes mattered, and which derived features were most
          sensitive to processing decisions.]
        </p>
        </warning>

      </subsection>

      <subsection xml:id="subsec-process-implications">
        <title>What Trajectories Add Beyond Correctness</title>

        <p>
          Endpoint correctness treats all responses with the same submitted result
          as equivalent.  A validated trajectory can distinguish direct placement
          from repeated revision, early checking from delayed checking, omission
          from wrong-slot error, and short paths from excess movement.
        </p>

        <warning>
        <p>
          [STUB: Interpret the primary RQ3 finding at the level warranted by the
          data.  Describe observable process differences and their possible
          instructional relevance, while presenting cognitive explanations as
          hypotheses requiring interviews, think-alouds, or experimental evidence.]
        </p>
        </warning>

      </subsection>

      <subsection xml:id="subsec-portability">
        <title>Portability and Environmental Dependence</title>

        <p>
          The validated implementation is environment-specific at its input
          boundaries: it extracts item structure from PreTeXt <c>cardsort</c>
          source and decodes Runestone's DataShop-formatted event conventions.
          The reconstruction and feature layers operate on canonical item and event
          objects after those transformations.  This architecture supports
          extension to other discrete card-to-zone environments, but a new
          environment requires an adapter and independent validation of its item
          and event semantics.
        </p>

        <p>
          The framework should not be generalized without modification to
          continuous spatial placement, resizing, rotation, nested objects, graph
          construction, or other drag-and-drop interactions that are not finite
          assignments of labeled cards to discrete zones.
        </p>
      </subsection>

      <subsection xml:id="subsec-limitations">
        <title>Limitations</title>

        <p>
          The empirical evidence comes from [ONE INSTITUTION OR CONTEXT] and a
          limited set of [N_ITEMS] authored items.  The number of learners may
          support stable within-item estimates, but the items are not a random
          sample of card-to-zone designs.  Item-level associations therefore do
          not establish general design effects.
        </p>

        <p>
          Internal check anchors validate replay only at submitted states, and
          attempts with no comparable check cannot receive the same level of
          verification.  Segmentation and active-time rules remain analytic
          choices.  Behavioral traces do not uniquely identify cognition, and the
          current study does not provide interview or think-aloud evidence.
          Finally, platform portability has been specified architecturally but not
          demonstrated with a second environment.
        </p>

